\paragraph{Mechanistic interpretation.}
The dose-response curve reveals concentrated routing: steep rise through 12 heads with diminishing returns beyond 20, where individual control heads are stronger but instruction heads collectively dominate, consistent with distributed many-weak-head routing.
Token-level specificity (KL ratio $1.56\times$; Table~\ref{tab:specificity_control}) is compressed by the 7-D action bottleneck; counterfactual ordering follows semantic distance, with L1 ablation consistent with signal removal.

\paragraph{Task-dependent routing.}
The routing effect is task-dependent: instruction-discriminative tasks show $2.5\times$ larger $|\Delta R^2|$ under L1 ablation than visually-sufficient tasks (Mann-Whitney $U = 21.0$, $p = 0.033$; Table~\ref{tab:task_complexity}).
Sensitivity analysis confirms robustness: reclassifying one borderline task yields $6.3\times$ ($p = 0.008$).
For deployment, instruction routing integrity is a bottleneck specifically for tasks where the instruction disambiguates among similar motor programs (e.g., ``put bowl on plate'' vs.\ ``put bowl on stove''); L1 probes could serve as runtime monitors.

\paragraph{Limitations and scope.}

